<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu al-Muhajir Dinar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 682/683</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu al-Muhajir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinar">Dinar</a> was an Umayyad commander and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> from about 674 to 681. A freedman client of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, he replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> and sought alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, winning over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awraba">Awraba</a> chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusaila">Kusaila</a>, who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. When Uqba returned to command he kept Abu al-Muhajir in chains, and both men were killed in Kusaila's ambush near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biskra">Biskra</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu al-Muhajir may have been of Arab or Coptic origin. He was a follower of Maslama ibn Mukhallad, a member of the Ansar and one of Muhammad's companions, who freed him. The first Umayyad caliph, Muawiyah I, made Maslama governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a>, though Ifriqiya was so far his only in name, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> had made only temporary raids in that direction. His life is hard to reconstruct because the histories of the Umayyad conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> survive in two versions, those written before the 11th century and those written later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Maslama appointed him amir of the Umayyad forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a> of the Banu Quraish, advising him to relieve Uqba with due deference. This seems not to have happened: Uqba was shackled and imprisoned until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> asked to see him, and on leaving for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> he vowed to treat Abu al-Muhajir as he had been treated.</p><p>Abu al-Muhajir is said to have abandoned, or in some accounts destroyed, Uqba's camp at Qayrawan and built a new settlement two miles away, which much later histories call Tākarwān. He is said to have been the first amir to stay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> permanently rather than return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> between raids. The two traditions disagree on his conquests: 9th-century histories have him advance no further west than Mila, while those from the 11th century on credit him with taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazid-i">Yazid I</a> restored Uqba to command, and on his return Uqba kept his vow, forcing Abu al-Muhajir to go in chains on his expeditions. In 683 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusaila">Kusaila</a> ambushed Uqba's forces near Tehouda, the Roman fort of Thabudeos. Uqba is said to have offered to unchain him, but Abu al-Muhajir answered that he would rather die fighting in his chains. Both were killed with 300 of Uqba's cavalry, and he is buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Sidi Okba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> with the dead of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vescera">Battle of Vescera</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu al-Muhajir Dinar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Arab writers say Kusaila was captured by the commander <b>Abu al-Muhajir</b>, converted to Islam and accompanied the Arab army, but was humiliated by Uqba ibn Nafi.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-altava">Kingdom of Altava</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Tabuda, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.83,5.73&amp;year=673" aria-label="Tabuda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Abu al-Muhajir Dinar"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5402 4577h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5402 4577h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.83,5.73&amp;year=673">What was here in 673 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
